---
import PracticeLayout from '../../layouts/PracticeLayout.astro';
import CrossLinkChip from '../../components/CrossLinkChip.astro';
import WarningCallout from '../../components/WarningCallout.astro';
---

<PracticeLayout
  title="Location Data"
  description="Your location is collected continuously, stored indefinitely, and sold. How it happens, who buys it, and what they do with it."
  subtitle="Your location is collected continuously, stored indefinitely, and sold."
  lastVerified="March 23, 2026"
  platformCount={4}
  severity="critical"
  severityText="Location data is among the most sensitive categories of personal information. A location history reveals home address, workplace, medical providers, religious attendance, political activity, and social relationships. It is collected continuously by smartphones, sold by app developers to data brokers, and purchased by advertisers, hedge funds, and government agencies."
>

  <!-- WHAT IT IS -->
  <div slot="what-it-is">
    <p>Location data tracking refers to the collection, storage, and commercial use of your physical location. This happens through multiple mechanisms: GPS (precise coordinates from your device's satellite receiver), IP address inference (city or neighborhood level, no permission required), Wi-Fi positioning (location triangulated from nearby Wi-Fi networks), Bluetooth beacons (precise indoor location in retail stores and venues), and cell tower triangulation (approximate location from mobile network data).</p>

    <p>The collection happens at two levels. The first is direct: apps you have granted location permission to that send your coordinates to their servers. The second is indirect: apps that include third-party SDKs from analytics or advertising companies, which collect location data on behalf of those companies without the user realizing the data is going to a third party.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY IT MATTERS -->
  <div slot="why-it-matters">
    <p>Location data is uniquely revealing because it is continuous and behavioral rather than self-reported. Other data collection captures what you say. Location data captures what you do.</p>

    <p>A location history that covers a year of a typical person's life reveals: home address and household members (from overnight stay patterns), workplace and employer (from daytime location patterns), which doctors and specialists they visit (from medical facility visits), whether they attend religious services and where (from weekly location patterns), whether they have visited a legal clinic, a mental health facility, an addiction treatment center, or an abortion provider, which political events or protests they attended, who they spend time with (from co-location patterns), and travel patterns including airports and hotels.</p>

    <WarningCallout title="Location data is sold to government agencies">
      <p>This data is collected at scale by a category of companies called location data brokers — Foursquare, SafeGraph, Veraset, and others — and sold to advertisers, hedge funds using foot traffic data for investment decisions, and government agencies including the military and immigration enforcement. Purchase of location data by government agencies allows warrantless surveillance of movement patterns that would otherwise require legal process.</p>
    </WarningCallout>
  </div>

  <!-- HOW IT WORKS -->
  <div slot="how-it-works">
    <ol class="steps-list">
      <li>
        <strong>A developer builds a mobile app and includes a third-party SDK.</strong> The SDK may be from an analytics or monetization company. The developer includes it to generate revenue or measure user behavior.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The SDK requests location permission on behalf of the app.</strong> The user sees a prompt from the app — not the SDK provider — and grants access. The user has consented to the app receiving their location, not necessarily to the SDK provider collecting it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>When the app is open (and in some cases running in the background), the SDK collects precise GPS coordinates.</strong> These are transmitted to the SDK provider's servers along with a device identifier.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The SDK provider aggregates location data from millions of devices</strong> across all the apps using their SDK, creating a persistent location history for each device.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The location data is sold to downstream buyers:</strong> advertisers (for geo-targeted campaigns), hedge funds (for retail foot traffic analysis), insurance companies (for risk modeling), and in some documented cases, government agencies (for surveillance without a warrant).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Buyers can reconstruct a device's movement history</strong> with precision sufficient to identify home address, workplace, and behavioral patterns — without ever knowing the device owner's name.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>


  <!-- WHO HAS YOUR DATA -->
  <div slot="who-has-data">
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Company</th>
            <th>What They Do</th>
            <th>Known Buyers</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Foursquare</td>
            <td>Operates the Pilgrim SDK, embedded in thousands of apps. Sells foot traffic data, place visit data, and audience segments derived from movement patterns.</td>
            <td>Advertisers, retail brands, marketers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>SafeGraph</td>
            <td>Sells location data to businesses, researchers, and government agencies. Has provided location data to the CDC and various state governments.</td>
            <td>Government, research, advertisers, hedge funds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Veraset</td>
            <td>Location data broker focused on enterprise and government clients.</td>
            <td>Law enforcement, immigration agencies</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>X-Mode (Outlogic)</td>
            <td>Collected data from prayer and Muslim community apps, among others. Data was sold to US military contractors. Now operating as Outlogic.</td>
            <td>US military contractors, government</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>NinthDecimal / Precisely</td>
            <td>Sells audience segments derived from physical movement patterns to advertisers.</td>
            <td>Advertisers, marketing agencies</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- HOW TO TURN IT OFF -->
  <div slot="how-to-turn-off">
    <WarningCallout title="App permissions are not a complete solution">
      <p>Disabling location permissions for apps on your device is the most effective control available, but it is incomplete. IP-based location inference requires no permission and cannot be disabled at the app level. Apps that use Wi-Fi positioning may continue to collect approximate location even without GPS access. The only way to prevent IP-based inference is a VPN, which introduces its own privacy tradeoffs.</p>
    </WarningCallout>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">iPhone</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Settings &rarr; Privacy &amp; Security &rarr; Location Services.</li>
        <li>Set each app to "Never" or "While Using" — avoid "Always."</li>
        <li>Where the app allows it, disable "Precise Location." Weather and transit apps do not require GPS-level precision.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Android</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>Settings &rarr; Location &rarr; App permissions.</li>
        <li>Review each app. Set to "Allow only while using the app" or "Deny" where location is not required.</li>
        <li>Toggle "Use precise location" off for apps that do not require GPS precision.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Google Location History</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>myaccount.google.com &rarr; Data &amp; privacy &rarr; Location History.</li>
        <li>Click "Turn off" to stop the persistent timeline log.</li>
        <li>Note: this does not stop Google from inferring your location during active Maps sessions or from IP address.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">TikTok</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>In your phone settings, revoke location permission from TikTok entirely.</li>
        <li>TikTok &rarr; Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Location &rarr; set to Never.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="turnoff-group">
      <h3 class="turnoff-heading">Facebook / Instagram</h3>
      <ol class="turnoff-steps">
        <li>In your phone settings, revoke location permission from Meta apps.</li>
        <li>Set location access to "Never" or "While Using" — not "Always."</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- RELATED PRACTICES -->
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/off-platform-tracking" label="Off-Platform Activity Tracking" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/third-party-sharing" label="Third-Party Data Sharing" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/data-brokers" label="Data Brokers" type="practice" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-practices" href="/practices/ad-personalization" label="Ad Personalization" type="practice" />

  <!-- RELATED PLATFORMS -->
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/google" label="Google" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/amazon" label="Amazon" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/tiktok" label="TikTok" type="platform" />
  <CrossLinkChip slot="related-platforms" href="/platforms/meta" label="Meta / Instagram" type="platform" />

</PracticeLayout>

